    Next, we prove that $\operatorname{Im} (w)$ is also bounded from below. By \eqref{theorem:germ:k1} and \eqref{theorem:germ:k2}, we have $|(G^{\circ k})'(p_0)|\simeq 1$ for all $k\in \mathbb{N}$. By Koebe's distortion theorem \cite{Pommerenke:conformal}*{Theorem 1.3} we have $|(G^{\circ k})'(w)|\simeq 1$ for all $k\in \mathbb{N}$ and for all $w\in B$. Let $r>0$ such that $B(w,r)\subset \mathbb H_{>R}\cap \mathbb H$ for each $w\in J^+(1)$.  Let $w\in J^+(1)$ be arbitrary. We apply Koebe's distortion theorem \cite{Pommerenke:conformal}*{Corollary 1.4} to the conformal map $G^{\circ k}|_{B(w,r)}$, which takes values in $\mathbb H_{>R}\cap \mathbb H$, and we obtain 
    $$\operatorname{dist}(G^{\circ k}(w), \mathbb{R}) \geq \operatorname{dist}(G^{\circ k}(w) , \partial G^{\circ k}(B(w,r))) \simeq r |(G^{\circ k})'(w)|\simeq 1$$
    for every $k\in \mathbb{N}$.  This proves that $\operatorname{dist}(J^+(k),\mathbb{R})\gtrsim 1$ for each $k\in \mathbb{N}$. Thus, $\operatorname{dist}(\phi(J^+),\mathbb{R})\gtrsim 1$, as desired.

    Therefore, $\phi(J^+)$ is between two horizontal lines $\operatorname{Im}(w)=\tau_1$ and $\operatorname{Im}(w)=\tau_2$, where $0<\tau_1<\tau_2$. If $\tau\in \mathbb{R}$ is fixed and  $\operatorname{Im} (\phi (x+iy))=\tau$, note that as $x\to \infty$ we have $y=o(x)$. A direct computation shows that 
        $$y=\frac{ -a \tau }{x^{n-1}}(1+o(1)).$$
    This proves that there exists $L\geq 1$ such that
        $$ J^+\subset \{(x,y)\in \mathbb{R}^2:  L^{-1} (1+|x|)^{-n+1} \leq y\leq L (1+|x|)^{-n+1}\},$$
    as illustrated in Figure \ref{fig:asymptotics}.
    The proof is complete.

Since critical points on the Julia set are pre-periodic, by Lemma \ref{lemma:no_infinite} \ref{lemma:no_infinite:2}, there are at most finitely many simply connected Fatou components that contain a critical point on their boundary. Choose $k_0\geq 1$ so that whenever a critical point lies in the closure of a simply connected Fatou component $\Omega$, the pre-period of $\Omega$ is at most $k_0$. Let $U,V$ be two disjoint Fatou components as in the statement such that $\overline{U}\cap \overline{V}=\{a_0\}$. Without loss of generality, we assume that $l_U \leq l_V$. If $l_V<k_0$, note that there are finitely many pairs $U,V$ with $l_U\leq l_V<k_0$. Thus, if $U,V$ are quasiconformally circularizable, then they are $L$-quasiconformally circularizable for some uniform $L\geq 1$. Now, we suppose that $l_V \geq  k_0$ and let $m=l_V-k_0$.  We set $V_m=f^{\circ m}(V)$, $U_m=f^{\circ m}(U)$, and $a_m=f^{\circ m}(a_0)$. Note that $l_{V_m}=k_0$ and $l_{U_m}\leq k_0$. As there are only finitely many Fatou components of pre-period $k_0$, by Theorem \ref{theorem:tangent} it suffices to prove that the map 
    \begin{align}\label{proposition:circularizable_gf:claim}
        \operatorname{id}: (\partial V\setminus \{a_0\}, d_{V, U}) \rightarrow (\partial V \setminus \{a_0\}, \chi)\,\,\, \text{is $\eta$-quasi-M\"obius}
    \end{align}
    for some $\eta$ depending only on $V_m$. In the following arguments, we use the term ``uniformly" to signify that a statement holds without dependence on $U,V,m$, but only on $V_m$ and on some constructions given below that depend on $V_m$.

    By assumption, $V_m$ is a Jordan region. Since $V_m$ is not periodic, $\overline{V_{m}}$ contains no parabolic periodic points by Lemma \ref{lemma:no_infinite} \ref{lemma:no_infinite:1}. Thus, $\overline{V_m}$ is disjoint from the accumulation points of the post-critical set. Recall that the post-critical set is the closure of the post-critical orbit ${\bigcup_{c\in \operatorname{Crit}(f)}\bigcup_{n\geq 1}f^{\circ n}(c)}$.  By the choice of $k_0$, $\overline{V_m}$ has a positive distance from the post-critical set. Consider a  Jordan region $N\supset \overline{V_{m}}$ so that $\overline{N}$ is disjoint from the post-critical set of $f$ and $N$ does not contain the entire boundary of any other Fatou component of pre-period at most $k_0$. Let $W$ be a Jordan region Fatou component with pre-period $l_W\leq k_0$, so $\partial W\not\subset N$. Due to the local connectivity of $\partial W$, at most finitely many components of $N\cap \partial W$ can intersect $\overline{V_m}$. Let $J\subset \partial W$ be a component of $N\cap \partial W$ that intersects $\overline{V_m}$ in exactly one point $z_0$ and is a quasiarc, as in Figure \ref{fig:quadrilateral}. Using Rickman's theorem (Theorem \ref{theorem:rickman}), we can construct a quasicircle in $N$ that contains non-overlapping subarcs $I_1,I_2$ of $J$ with $z_0\in I_1\cap I_2$, intersects $\overline{V_m}$ only at $z_0$, and separates $\partial N$ from $V_m$. Let $Q$ be the quasidisk bounded by this quasicircle and which contains $V_m$. Consider the quadrilateral $R=Q\setminus \overline{V_m}$ with vertical sides $I_1,I_2$; see Figure \ref{fig:quadrilateral}. With this process, to each of the finitely many pairs $V_m,W$, where $l_{V_m}=k_0$ and $l_W\leq k_0$, and to each of the finitely many arcs $J$ as above we assign a quasidisk $Q$ and a quadrilateral $R=Q\setminus \overline{V_m}$ with $\overline{R}\subset N$.

