    Since $\overline{N}$ is disjoint from the post-critical set, there exists a Jordan region $N'\supset \overline{V}$ such that $f^{\circ m}\colon  N' \rightarrow N$ is conformal, and in fact it extends to a conformal map from a neighborhood of $\overline{N'}$ onto a neighborhood of $\overline{N}$. Thus, a component of $\partial U_m\cap N$ is an arc $J$ such that $J\cap \overline{V_m}=\{a_m\}$. The pair $U,V$ is quasiconformally circularizable, so $\partial U$ is a quasicircle. Since $f^{\circ m}$ is bi-Lipschitz in $\overline{N'}$, the set $J$ is a quasiarc. Thus, the triple $V_m,U_m,J$ corresponds to a quasidisk $Q$ and a quadrilateral $R$ among the ones constructed above; see Figure~\ref{fig:quadrilateral}. Let $Q',R'$ be their preimages under $f^{\circ m}$. By conformality, we have $\operatorname{Mod} R'=\operatorname{Mod} R$. 


    Since the pair $U,V$ is quasiconformally circularizable and $f^{\circ m}$ is conformal in a neighborhood of $a_0$, by Lemma \ref{lemma:tangent_local} we see that the pair $V_m,Q^*$, where $Q^*=\widehat \mathbb{C}\setminus \overline{Q}$, is quasiconformally circularizable with uniform constants, given that we have constructed a finite collection of quasidisks like $Q$.

    By Proposition~\ref{prop:comparisonhyperbolicmetric}, for $z \in V$, the hyperbolic metric $\rho_{U^*}(z)|dz|$ is comparable to $\rho_{Q'}(z)|dz|$, depending only on $\operatorname{Mod} R$, and therefore with uniform constants. Hence, 
    $$
    \operatorname{id}\colon (\partial V\setminus \{a_0\}, d_{V, U}) \to (\partial V \setminus \{a_0\}, d_{V, (Q')^*})
    $$ is uniformly bi-Lipschitz; recall the definition of $d_{V,U}$ from Section \ref{section:uniformization}. On the other hand, since the map $f^{\circ m}\colon  Q' \rightarrow Q$ is conformal, the map 
    $$
    f^{\circ m}\colon (\partial V\setminus \{a_0\}, d_{V, (Q')^*}) \to (\partial V_{m} \setminus \{a_{m}\}, d_{V_m, Q^*})
    $$
    is an isometry. Since the pair $V_m,Q^*$ is quasiconformally circularizable, by Theorem~\ref{theorem:tangent} the map 
    $$\operatorname{id}\colon  (\partial V_{m}\setminus \{a_{m}\}, d_{V_{m}, Q^*}) \to (\partial V_{m}\setminus \{a_{m}\} , \chi)$$
    is uniformly quasi-M\"obius. By Lemma \ref{lemma:koebe_qm}, which is an implication of Koebe's distortion theorem, the conformality of the map $f^{\circ m}\colon N' \to N$ implies that 
    $$
    (f^{\circ m})^{-1}\colon (\partial V_{m} \setminus \{a_{m}\} , \chi ) \to (\partial V\setminus \{a_0\}, \chi)
    $$
    is  uniformly quasi-M\"obius. Altogether, the composition of the above maps is
    $$\operatorname{id}\colon (\partial V\setminus \{a_0\}, d_{V, U}) \rightarrow (\partial V\setminus \{a_0\}, \chi)$$ 
    and therefore it is uniformly quasi-M\"obius, which proves the desired \eqref{proposition:circularizable_gf:claim}.

\label{remark:prop:quasidisk}
    Note that if $U$ is a quasidisk that is an immediate basin of a parabolic periodic point $a_0$, then condition \ref{prop:quasidisk:pereq:2} implies that the multiplicity of $a_0$ is at least $3$. 
    See Figure~\ref{fig:JuliaSet} for an illustration of the non-local homeomorphism condition.

By Lemma \ref{lemma:no_infinite} \ref{lemma:no_infinite:2} there exists a number $k_0\geq 1$ so that whenever a critical point lies in the closure of a simply connected Fatou component $\Omega$, the pre-period $l_\Omega$ of $\Omega$ is at most $k_0$. Without loss of generality, we assume that $l_U \leq l_V$. Since there are only finitely many Fatou components of pre-period at most $k_0$, we may assume that $l_V \geq  k_0$. For $m=l_V-k_0$ we set $V_m=f^{\circ m}(V)$ and $U_m=f^{\circ m}(U)$. Then $l_{V_m}=k_0$ and $l_{U_m}\leq k_0$. 

    As in the proof of Proposition \ref{proposition:circularizable_gf}, there exists a Jordan region $N\supset \overline{V_m}$ so that $\overline{N}$ is disjoint from the post-critical set of $f$ and $N$ does not contain the boundary of any Fatou component of pre-period at most $k_0$. Let $W$ be a Jordan region Fatou component with pre-period $l_W\leq k_0$, so $\partial  W\not\subset N$. Due to the local connectivity of $\partial W$, if $N\cap \partial W$ has infinitely many components, their diameters must converge to $0$. Thus, if $N_1$ is a Jordan region with $\overline{V_m}\subset N_1\subset \overline{N_1}\subset N$, then at most finitely many components of $N\cap \partial W$ can intersect $N_1$. By shrinking $N_1$, we can ensure that the only components of $N\cap \partial W$ that intersect $N_1$, if any, are the ones that also intersect $\overline{V_m}$. Let $A=N_1\setminus \overline{V_m}$. With this process, to each of the finitely many pairs $V_m,W$, where $l_{V_m}=k_0$ and $l_W\leq k_0$, we associate an annulus $A$, so that if a component of $N\cap \partial W$ intersects $A$, then it also intersects $\overline{V_m}$. See Figure \ref{fig:annulus} for an illustration.

