    There exists a Jordan region $N'\supset \overline{V}$ such that $g=f^{\circ m}\colon N'\to N$ is conformal. Let $N_1'=g^{-1}(N_1)$ and $A'= g^{-1}(A)$. Since $g\colon A' \to A$ is conformal, we have $\operatorname{Mod} A' = \operatorname{Mod} A$. 

    We claim that the annulus $A'$ is disjoint from $U$. Suppose instead that $A'\cap U\neq\emptyset$. Since $\overline{U}\cap \overline{V}=\emptyset$, we have $A'\setminus U\neq \emptyset$. By the connectedness of $A'$, we obtain $\partial U\cap A'\neq \emptyset$. Thus, there exists a component $J'$ of $\partial U\cap N'$ such that $J'\cap A'\neq \emptyset$. Note that $J'\cap \overline{V}=\emptyset$. This implies that there exists a component $J=g(J')$ of $N\cap \partial U_m$ such that $J\cap A\neq \emptyset$ and $J\cap \overline{V_m}=\emptyset$. This contradicts the properties of the annulus $A$. Therefore, $A'\cap U=\emptyset$. 

    It follows that $A'$ separates $\overline{U}$ and $\overline{V}$, so $\operatorname{Mod} A(\overline{U}, \overline{V}) \geq \operatorname{Mod} A' =\operatorname{Mod} A$. Since $A$ is an annulus among a finite collection of annuli constructed above (corresponding to the various pairs $V_m,W$), we see that $\operatorname{Mod} A(\overline{U},\overline{V})$ is uniformly bounded from below, as desired.

\label{theorem:uniformization_general}
    Let $f$ be a geometrically finite rational map whose Julia set is not a Jordan curve.
    Let $\{U_i\}_{i\in I}$ be a collection of at least two Fatou components of $f$ whose forward orbits consist of Jordan regions. The following are equivalent.
    \begin{enumerate}[label=\normalfont(\arabic*)]
        \item\label{theorem:uniformization_general:1} (Quasiconformal Uniformization) There exists a quasiconformal map $g$ of $\widehat \mathbb{C}$ that maps $U_i$ to a disk for each $i\in I$ and is $1$-quasiconformal in $\widehat \mathbb{C}\setminus \bigcup_{i\in I}U_i$.
        \item\label{theorem:uniformization_general:2} (Dynamical criterion) 
        For each $i\in I$ the Fatou component $U_i$ is a quasidisk and if $\overline{U_i}\cap \overline{U_j}\neq \emptyset$ for some $i\neq j$ then $\overline{U_i}\cap \overline{U_j}$ consists of a single point $a_0$ that satisfies one of the following alternatives.
        \begin{itemize}
            \item $a_0$ is mapped to a parabolic periodic cycle of multiplicity $3$ by an iterate of $f$ that is a local homeomorphism near $a_0$.
            \item $a_0$ is mapped to a parabolic periodic cycle of multiplicity $2$ by an iterate of $f$ that is locally two-to-one near $a_0$.
        \end{itemize}
    \end{enumerate}
     Moreover, in this case the map $g|_{\widehat \mathbb{C}\setminus \bigcup_{i\in I}U_i}$ is unique up to post-composition with conformal automorphisms of $\widehat \mathbb{C}$.

By conjugating $f$ with a M\"obius transformation, we assume that $\infty$ lies in the Fatou set. Let $a_0\in \overline{U_1}\cap \overline{U_2}$ be an isolated point. If the orbit of $a_0$ contains critical points or parabolic points, then it is pre-periodic since $f$ is geometrically finite. 
    Suppose that the orbit of $a_0$ does not contain critical points or parabolic points. By Sullivan's theorem, the components $U_1,U_2$ are eventually periodic. By passing to an iterate, we assume that $f(U_1)$ and $f(U_2)$ are fixed Fatou components, not necessarily distinct. Then by \cite{McMullen:Hausdorff_GF}*{Theorem 6.5} the point $a_0$ lies in the \textit{radial Julia set} of $f$. By definition, this means that there exists $r>0$ such that for every $\varepsilon>0$ there exists a neighborhood $\Omega$ of $a_0$ and $n\in \mathbb{N}$ such that $\operatorname{diam}(\Omega)<\varepsilon$ and the map 
    $$f^{\circ n}\colon \Omega\to B(f^{\circ n}(a_0),r)$$
    is conformal. By assumption, if $\Omega$ is sufficiently small, then $a_0$ is the unique point of $\overline{U_1}\cap \overline{U_2}$ in $\Omega$. Also, by shrinking $r$ we may assume that $\operatorname{diam} f(U_i)>2r$, so $f(U_i)$ is not contained in $B(f^{\circ n}(a_0),r)$ for $i=1,2$.

    By \cite{LeiYongcheng:local_connectivity}*{Lemma 2.1}, $U_1,U_2$ and $f(U_1),f(U_2)$ have locally connected boundary. Let $I$ be the infinite set of indices $n\in \mathbb{N}$ such that there exists a neighborhood $\Omega_n$ of $a_0$ with $\Omega_n\cap \overline{U_1}\cap \overline{U_2}=\{a_0\}$ and the map $f_n=f^{\circ n}\colon \Omega_n\to B(a_n,r)$ is conformal, where $a_n=f^{\circ n}(a_0)$. Consider conformal maps $\phi_i\colon \mathbb{D}\to U_i$, $i=1,2$, which extend continuously to the boundaries by Carath\'eodory's theorem. For $n\in I$ and $i=1,2$, let $P_{i,n}\subset \partial \mathbb{D}$ be the union of the components of $\phi_i^{-1}(\Omega_n\cap \partial U_i)$ that intersect $\phi_i^{-1}(a_0)$. Also, let $\psi_i\colon \mathbb{D}\to f(U_i)$, $i=1,2$, be conformal maps. Let $i\in \{1,2\}$. Consider the conformal map $g_i=\psi_i^{-1}\circ f_n\circ \phi_i$ defined in a neighborhood of $P_{i,n}$ in $\mathbb{D}$. Note that $g_i$ extends continuously to $P_{i,n}$ and let $Q_{i,n}=g(P_{i,n})$. Each component of $Q_{i,n}$ intersects $\psi_i^{-1}(a_n)$ and is mapped by $\psi_i$ to a curve that passes through $a_n$ and has endpoints on $\partial B(a_n,r)$; see Figure \ref{fig:isolated}. By the uniform continuity of $\psi_i$ in $\overline{\mathbb{D}}$, there exists a uniform constant $\delta>0$ such that for $J_{i,n}=\psi_i^{-1}(a_n)\cap Q_{i,n}$ we have
    \begin{align}\label{lem:isolated:delta}
        \operatorname{dist}(J_{i,n}, \partial \mathbb{D}\setminus Q_{i,n})\geq \delta.
    \end{align}


